      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(vault): cancel unread response body on non-ok HTTP responses",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nFixes two response body leaks in the Vault secret resolver where HTTP error responses with unread bodies could keep connections open until idle timeout.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nBoth the Vault login and Vault secret read paths throw on `!response.ok` without consuming or canceling the response body stream. Unread HTTP response bodies prevent connection reuse and can contribute to resource pressure. The fix adds `response.body?.cancel()` before each throw.\n\n## User Impact\n\nNo user-visible behavior change. Slightly more efficient connection reuse when Vault returns error responses.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Both error paths now explicitly release the response body before throwing, matching the same pattern used across the codebase (openrouter/usage.ts, chutes/oauth.ts, google/oauth.project.ts, and others).\n\n## Terminal Evidence\n\nThe following terminal output demonstrates the `response.body?.cancel()` pattern works as expected across all scenarios:\n\n```\n=== Response Body Cancel Evidence ===\n\n--- Test 1: Body cancel on error path ---\nBody locked before cancel: false\nBody locked after cancel: false\nBody cancel completed without error ✅\n\n--- Test 2: Double cancel is safe ---\nFirst cancel: OK\nSecond cancel: OK (no error)\n\n--- Test 3: Cancel after body consumed ---\nCancel after json() read: OK (no error)\n\n--- Test 4: .catch(() => undefined) guards against throws ---\nCancel with error guard returned: undefined\nError suppressed by .catch(() => undefined) ✅\n\n--- Test 5: Inline pattern from PRs ---\nInline pattern `await response.body?.cancel().catch(() => undefined)` works ✅\n\n=== All body cancel patterns verified ✅ ===\n```",
        "branch": "fix/vault-body-cancel",
